<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lagash</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lagash was an ancient Sumerian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. Under rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudea">Gudea</a>, it flourished in the period between the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> and the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a>. It was subsequently absorbed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire</a>, and Gudea's statues and inscriptions are among the best-known works of Sumerian art.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lagash.jpg" alt="Lagash" width="320" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marie-Lan Nguyen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Relief_Ur-Nanshe_Louvre_AO2344.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lagash, modern al-Hiba in Dhi Qar Governorate, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, lay northwest of the junction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> and east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>. The Lagash state incorporated three cities: Lagash itself; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Girsu</a> (modern Telloh), about 25 kilometres to the northwest, the religious centre with its main temple, the E-ninnu, dedicated to the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ningirsu">Ningirsu</a>; and Nina (Tell Zurghul), which marked the state's southern limit.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Significant occupation began early in the 3rd millennium BCE, in the Early Dynastic I period (c. 2900–2600 BCE), and peaked at about 500 hectares in Early Dynastic III (c. 2500–2334 BCE), the age of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty</a> of Lagash. Ur-Nanshe left inscriptions mostly about temple building and claimed to have conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a>. His grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eannatum">Eannatum</a> made Lagash a major power across much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, though many of the realms he conquered often revolted.</p><p>A long border dispute with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a> over the fertile Guʾedena plain is told on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stele-of-the-vultures">Stele of the Vultures</a>, dated about 2450 BCE, which shows one of the earliest organised battles known, with Lagash's army advancing in a dense <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a>. The last ruler of the dynasty, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urukagina">Urukagina</a>, known for judicial, social and economic reforms, was defeated by Lugalzagesi.</p><p>About 2300 BCE Sargon of Akkad laid waste the land from Lagash to the sea, and Lagash passed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rimush">Rimush</a> faced revolts and reconquered Lagash among other cities; for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> and Lagash he recorded 8,049 killed, 5,460 captured and enslaved and 5,985 expelled and annihilated. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a> fell, a Second Dynasty (c. 2260 – c. 2023 BCE) won full independence, and under Gudea Lagash entered a period of riches and power. Thousands of inscriptions and many statues survive from his reign, among them the Gudea cylinders, which hold the longest known text in Sumerian.</p><p>Nam-mahani, the last ruler of the dynasty, was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nammu">Ur-Nammu</a>, first ruler of the Ur III empire, whose law code names him as slain; statues of Nam-mahani and his wife were beheaded. Under Ur, Lagash, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Girsu</a> and Nigin formed the largest and most prosperous province, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-vizier">Grand Vizier</a>, the second highest official of the empire, lived there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The site is one of the largest archaeological sites in the region, about 3.5 kilometres from north to south and 1.5 from east to west. Robert Koldewey excavated it for six weeks in 1887, Thorkild Jacobsen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuad-safar">Fuad Safar</a> identified it as Lagash in 1953, and a team from the Metropolitan Museum of Art and New York University worked there for five seasons between 1968 and 1976. After the Old Babylonian period it was largely deserted until a Seleucid fortress was built in the 2nd century BCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lagash” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ur-Nammu consolidated control over Sumerian cities such as Uruk, <b>Lagash</b> and Nippur and re-established Sumerian political and cultural pre-eminence after a period of foreign domination.</blockquote><a href="/empire/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The office survived every change of master: Naram-Sin appointed Enmenana, Ur-Bau of <b>Lagash</b> installed Enanepada and Ur-Nammu appointed Ennirgalana.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Conflict is better documented in the Early Dynastic period, c. 2900–2350 BCE, when Uruk competed with Kish, Ur, <b>Lagash</b> and Umma for land, water and supremacy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uruk">Uruk · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Sumerian was the language of Sumer, the land of the great cities of southern Mesopotamia, such as Uruk, Ur, <b>Lagash</b>, Nippur, Eridu and Umma, between the lower courses of the Tigris and Euphrates.</blockquote><a href="/language/sumerian">Sumerian · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Akkadian Empire, claimed that ships from Meluhha, Magan and Dilmun moored at the quay of his capital, Akkad, and Gudea, ruler of <b>Lagash</b> in the late 3rd millennium BCE, wrote of lapis lazuli and bright carnelian from Meluhha; other texts mention Meluhhan gold dust and fine woods.</blockquote><a href="/culture/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>By the Early Dynastic period (c. 2900–2350 BCE) a dozen or more independent cities, among them Kish, Ur, <b>Lagash</b>, Umma and Uruk itself, shared the plain.</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
